@node D-Bus Signals
@section Signals

cmacs's D-Bus subsystem exposes three kinds of signals.  Phase 1
ships the @emph{infrastructure} for all of them; Phase 2's
per-resource modules start @emph{automatically firing}
property-changed and lifecycle signals.

@subsection Standard signals

@subsubheading PropertiesChanged

Defined by @code{org.freedesktop.DBus.Properties}.  Fires whenever a
property value changes on any object that has registered properties.

@example
Body signature: sa@{sv@}as
Body fields:    iface name, changed dict, invalidated names array
@end example

Phase 1 doesn't yet attach Properties to any path (no module
declares a property), so this signal isn't fired.  Phase 2 starts
firing it from per-buffer / per-frame / per-window objects.  The
Lisp DEFUN @code{cmacs-dbus-emit-properties-changed} is available
now so module authors can wire it up:

@example
(cmacs-dbus-emit-properties-changed
 "/org/cmacs/Editor/Buffer/12"
 "org.cmacs.Editor1.Buffer.Resource"
 (list :modified t :point 1234)
 nil)
@end example

@subsubheading InterfacesAdded / InterfacesRemoved

Defined by @code{org.freedesktop.DBus.ObjectManager}.  Fire when a
managed child appears or disappears.  Phase 1 registers the iface,
but the child registry stays empty until Phase 2.

@subsection Custom signals

The DEFUN @code{cmacs-dbus-emit-signal} lets any elisp code
broadcast a typed signal:

@example
(cmacs-dbus-emit-signal
 "/org/cmacs/Editor"
 "org.cmacs.Editor1.Hooks"
 "BufferSaved"
 (list (or (buffer-file-name) "") (buffer-name) 42))
@end example

The @code{PARAMS} list is marshalled into a tuple of best-fit
GVariant types: nil → @code{""}, t → bool, fixnum → int64,
float → double, string → string, symbol → string (name), list of
strings → @code{as}, list of mixed → @code{av}.

@subsection Subscribing to signals

@subsubheading From the shell

@example
gdbus monitor --session --dest org.cmacs.Editor

# Filter:
gdbus monitor --session --dest org.cmacs.Editor \
    | grep -E 'PropertiesChanged|InterfacesAdded|BufferSaved'
@end example

@subsubheading From Python (dasbus)

@example
from dasbus.connection import SessionMessageBus
from dasbus.loop import EventLoop

bus = SessionMessageBus()
def on_props(iface, changed, invalidated):
    print(f"@{iface@}: @{dict(changed)@}")

editor = bus.get_proxy("org.cmacs.Editor", "/org/cmacs/Editor")
editor.PropertiesChanged.connect(on_props)
EventLoop().run()
@end example

@subsection Signal-emit lifecycle

Every emit goes through @code{g_dbus_connection_emit_signal} on the
cmacs GMainContext.  Things to know:

@itemize @bullet
@item
@strong{Fire-and-forget.} D-Bus signals don't have replies.
Subscribers may or may not receive; cmacs doesn't track delivery.
@item
@strong{Service must be running.} @code{cmacs-dbus-emit-*} returns
nil silently when @code{cmacs-dbus-start} hasn't been called.
@item
@strong{Marshalling errors} an unsupported Lisp value in PARAMS
becomes an empty string rather than aborting.
@item
@strong{Order is not guaranteed across signals.} D-Bus delivers in
send-order @emph{per connection}, but external watchers see them
interleaved with method calls.
@end itemize
